Source: anoa
Section: web
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Eka Tresna Irawan <anak10thn@gmail.com>
# Qt 6.4 is the floor the build declares (find_package(Qt6 6.4) in
# CMakeLists.txt), and it is a real floor rather than a cautious one: Debian 12
# ships exactly 6.4.2 and Ubuntu 22.04 ships 6.2, which cannot build this at
# all. 22.04 users want the AppImage instead.
#
# libcups2-dev looks unrelated and is not: Qt6PrintSupport is a
# WebEngineWidgets dependency, and without it cmake fails with the misleading
# "Failed to find WebEngineWidgets".
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13),
               cmake (>= 3.16),
               qt6-base-dev (>= 6.4),
               qt6-webengine-dev (>= 6.4),
               qt6-websockets-dev (>= 6.4),
               libcups2-dev
Standards-Version: 4.6.2
Homepage: https://github.com/porcupine-md/anoa-browser
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: anoa
Architecture: any
# ${shlibs:Depends} covers everything linked, and the distribution's own
# qt6-webengine package carries what Chromium dlopen()s. That is the difference
# between this and the container image, which installs two dozen libraries by
# hand because it unpacks a tarball with Qt bundled inside it.
#
# A font is what remains: nothing links one and nothing declares one, and
# without it nothing renders as text — screenshots come back as boxes, which
# reads as a broken browser rather than a missing font.
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
         fonts-dejavu-core
Description: a real browser that takes commands
 anoa is Chromium in one binary, driven from a shell, an HTTP endpoint, any CDP
 client, or over MCP. There is no driver to install alongside it and no
 headless-versus-real distinction to reason about.
 .
 The browser is the session: every command attaches to a browser that is
 already running and leaves it running, so the page, the cookies and the scroll
 position survive between commands. That is what makes it usable one command at
 a time, by a process that starts and exits, rather than only from inside a
 long-lived script.
 .
 snapshot returns refs that later commands target, and clicks are hit-tested —
 a button underneath a consent banner is reported rather than clicked through.
 Both exist because the caller cannot see the screen.
